{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FbAXuj7SxnchmhqdHxiTKF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n2\t\n3\tUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\n4\tRepresentations 2026 submission.\n5\t\n6\t## Hard Constraints\n7\t- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n8\t- Submission type: conference paper.\n9\t- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n10\t- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n11\t- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n12\t- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n13\t- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n14\t- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n15\t  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n16\t  cause desk rejection.\n17\t- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n18\t\n19\t## Audience\n20\t- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n21\t  empirical methodology.\n22\t- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n23\t  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n24\t- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n25\t\n26\t## Framing Priorities\n27\t- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n28\t- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n29\t  and under which conditions.\n30\t- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n31\t- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n32\t- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n33\t- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n34\t- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n35\t\n36\t## Manuscript Guidance\n37\t- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n38\t- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n39\t- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n40\t  hyperparameters that matter.\n41\t- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n42\t- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n43\t- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n44\t  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n45\t- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n46\t\n47\t## Anti-Goals\n48\t- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n49\t- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n50\t- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n51\t- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n52\t- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n53\t- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n54\t\n55\t## Decision Rule\n56\t- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n57\t- Use this brief to choose emphasis, 